      "resolved_text": "— 표준노드링크 기반 송파–고성 사례의 쌍대 시뮬레이션 —"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "A Wartime-Assumption-Driven Road-Network Simulation",
      "anchor_mode": "contains",
      "anchor_style_name": null,
      "id": "title-en",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "paragraph_index": 2,
      "resolved_text": "Conditional Comparison of Reserve-Force Transport Alternatives under Resource and Network-Scope Constraints: A Paired Simulation Study on the Songpa–Goseong Corridor"
    },
    {
      "anchor": "Purpose. Wartime reserve-force mobilization",
      "anchor_mode": "contains",
      "anchor_style_name": null,
      "id": "abstract-purpose",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "paragraph_index": 7,
      "resolved_text": "Purpose. This study identifies conditional decision boundaries between bus-only and rail–bus multimodal transport for a reserve-force mobilization corridor. It asks how network reduction, unequal resource bundles, road and rail degradation, fleet scarcity, and response policies change the comparison."
    },
    {
      "anchor": "Methods. Wartime conditions are treated",
      "anchor_mode": "contains",
      "anchor_style_name": null,
      "id": "abstract-methods",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "paragraph_index": 8,
      "resolved_text": "Methods. A public-data-based conditional scenario simulation was built on the Korean Standard Node-Link network. Top-3, top-5, and top-10 corridor graphs were checked against the full road graph, and top-10 was used for the main experiments. Vehicles physically return through the reverse road network before reuse. Eight restartable campaigns generated 19,950 runs. Common-random-number pairs were analyzed with paired t intervals, 10,000-replicate percentile bootstrap intervals, a joint-seed bootstrap for the road-damage break-even point, a two-level bootstrap for random threat sets, and Morris elementary effects."
    },
    {
      "anchor": "Results. Completion saturates",
      "anchor_mode": "contains",
      "anchor_style_name": null,
      "id": "abstract-results",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "paragraph_index": 9,
      "resolved_text": "Results. Resource definition changed the baseline conclusion. With the configured bundles (23 road vehicles for bus-only versus 46 road vehicles plus rail for multimodal), mean completion times were 374.1 and 363.8 min; the paired difference of 10.3 min was unresolved by its 95% t interval [−12.6, 33.2]. With equal total road fleets of 23 vehicles, mean Δ(bus-only−multimodal) was −101.7 min, with a 95% t interval [−125.0, −78.4]. Under the equal-road-fleet frame and normally available rail, the estimated long-haul road-damage break-even multiplier was 1.742, with a joint-seed bootstrap interval [1.550, 2.003]. In the configured-bundle graph-scope check, top-5 and top-10 matched full-graph connectivity and policy ranking in the tested conditions; top-3 did not. In the equal-road-fleet frame, static multimodal transport failed to complete when rail was unavailable, while a precheck switching policy tied bus-only."
    },
    {
      "anchor": "Conclusion. Mode superiority",
      "anchor_mode": "contains",
      "anchor_style_name": null,
      "id": "abstract-conclusion",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "paragraph_index": 10,
      "resolved_text": "Conclusion. The contribution is a conditional decision boundary, not a general modal ranking. Comparisons must state the resource frame, network scope, rail state, and response rule. Results apply only to the specified public-data corridor and planning assumptions."
    },
    {
      "anchor": "Keywords:",
